<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madara Rider</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 8th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madara">Madara</a> Rider is a large rock relief carved about 23 metres up a cliff near the village of Madara in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, probably in the early 8th century. It shows a horseman spearing a lion, with a dog running behind, surrounded by Greek inscriptions recording events of the reigns of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> khans, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tervel">Tervel</a>. It is the best-known monument of pagan Bulgaria and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madara-rider.jpg" alt="Madara Rider" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Dom2002 assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JE%C5%B9DZIEC_Z_MADARY.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
